2026-08-03 | runtime-output-never-ignored | origin-repo-had-it-by-hand | maintainer | no-gap | `init` never wrote a `.gitignore`, so a consuming project committed `.ai/runs/` (worker transcripts), `.ai/.preflight` (the receipt) and `.ai/host.json` (per-machine dispatch config resolved from the committed `hosts.json`). The receipt is the dangerous one: it names a commit SHA and the push guard accepts it, so a committed receipt UNBLOCKS A PUSH on a machine that never ran the checks -- pulled, not earned. `.ai/STOP` likewise halts the runner and must never arrive by pull. Found 2026-08-03 by running init in a synthetic project, running preflight, and reading `git status`: three run artefacts staged for commit. `uninstall` then left `.ai/runs/` behind, because it listed the directory under remove-only-if-empty -- the rule that protects the operator's own work, applied to a directory holding nothing but ours. Why it was believed: the framework's own repo has had those four entries in its `.gitignore` since before the extraction, so every test, every gate run and every manual check inside it behaved correctly; the missing step is invisible from the one repo where the property is satisfied by hand. Same shape as `gate-scope-outlived-its-directory`: the framework repo is not a fair sample of the repos the framework runs in. Generalises: A PROPERTY THE ORIGIN REPO HAS BY HAND IS A PROPERTY THE TOOL IS NEVER ASKED TO PRODUCE -- the test for an install step has to run in a repo that lacks what the author's repo happens to have. Fixed: a `templates/gitignore` merged in like the other content-is-the-requirement files, `uninstall` gained a `trees` list removed with contents, and four tests assert the patterns land, the comments sit on their own lines (a `#` after a pattern is part of the pattern), an existing `.gitignore` keeps its own entries, and run output is gone afterwards.
2026-08-03 | install-ended-with-homework | work-printed-instead-of-done | maintainer | no-gap | `init` finished by printing five numbered commands for a HUMAN to run: commit, doctor, gates, preflight, runner --dry-run. Every one of those is reading check output and repairing what it names, which is precisely the job this framework exists to give to an agent -- in the origin project every gate violation for three months was read and fixed by one. So the closing screen was the framework declining to use itself, and the first install by someone who had not built it stopped at exactly that screen. Maintainer, 2026-08-03: "It's not meant to be used by a human, but used by AI. I would like the init to end with LLM running the diagnosis and fixing bugs that it found. Not human, who checks gates manually now. In this project, it is you, who uses the nightshift, does the preflight and fixes bugs that gates find." Why it was believed: the list was an improvement on what it replaced (a `your call:` section naming decisions with no way to act on them), so the fix for 'unactionable' was read as 'make each item actionable' rather than 'stop handing it over'. The measure of an install step is not whether a person CAN follow it. Generalises: A TOOL THAT ENDS BY PRINTING WORK HAS DECIDED WHO DOES IT, and if the answer contradicts what the tool is for, the printout is the bug. The tell is an imperative in the output with no subject -- 'run the gates' names the action and hides the actor. Fixed: `nightshift fix` runs every check via `preflight.run_checks`, dispatches one agent at the lead tier with the failures and their reproduction commands, re-diagnoses, and stops on green / no-progress / three rounds; its prompt carries the seven forbidden moves (delete the gate, raise the budget, xfail the test, --no-corrections, commit) because an agent told to make checks pass will weaken the check, and each of those has been done here in good faith. What it declines to decide becomes a `needs-