2026-08-03 | board-had-no-view | origin-repo-had-it-by-hand | maintainer | no-gap | `init` created seven lane directories and no way to look at them. The board's design is that a card's state IS the directory it sits in -- no database, no daemon, resumable from disk -- and that design is exactly why a directory tree is not a board to a human: seven folders of markdown is the storage format, not the interface. The origin project has had `Board.base`, an Obsidian Bases file giving a Kanban plus a Live and an Archive table, since long before the extraction; it was never a template, so no consuming project ever got one. The board README shipped by `init` says 'the Kanban' four times and never said where it came from, and `card_schema` even allows a `kanban_order` field on the grounds that 'Base Board writes it when a card is dragged' -- a gate accommodating a tool the installer did not install. Reported by the maintainer, 2026-08-03: he opened a freshly installed repo in Obsidian and there was no board. Why it was believed: every check the framework can run passes without it. Gates, preflight, the runner and the reconciler all read directories, so the entire test surface is blind to whether the board is legible to a person -- the missing piece is the only part of the board a human touches, and the only part nothing automated needs. Third instance of `origin-repo-had-it-by-hand` in one day (after `runtime-output-never-ignored` and `private-lane-never-created`), and the sharpest: the other two were files a check would eventually have caught. Generalises: WHAT THE ORIGIN REPO HAS THAT NO CHECK READS IS INVISIBLE TWICE OVER -- absent from the templates because nobody ported it, and absent from the failures because nothing tests it. The question to ask of any extraction is not only 'what does the code need' but 'what does the human open'. Fixed: `templates/board.base` rendered with `{{board}}` and `{{private_lane}}`, its `boardColumns` GENERATED from `board.LANES` in flow or