2026-08-03 | budget-without-its-subject | schema-field-nothing-read | maintainer | yes-now | `init` asked the operator for `[memory].budget_bytes` and wrote it, and never wrote `[memory].orientation` -- which is the field naming WHICH files the budget applies to. `orientation_budget` reads both, so with an empty orientation list it measured nothing and reported green: the interview question was answered, the answer landed in the manifest, and the gate it was for could not run. Measured 2026-08-03 on a fresh install: `orientation: ()`, `budget_bytes: 100000`, gate silent. The second half is the same failure at the doctrine level -- the origin project's whole orientation-vs-detail structure (small current-state files, dated narrative in companions loaded on demand) existed only as prose in that project's own CLAUDE.md, so a new repo got the machinery and none of the structure. Maintainer, 2026-08-03: 'I installed it in a new repo and it's claude.md is a mess, while we have nice rules and structure for dungeoneer... this probably should be a rule enforced by nightshift?' Why it was believed: `orientation` carries CONFIRM because 'these are the files every session loads' is a claim about the operator's OWN documents and genuinely unguessable -- correct for their files, and wrongly extended to the four memory stubs `init` writes itself, which are the always-loaded set BY CONSTRUCTION of the CLAUDE.md the same run ships. Not guessing is not the same as having nothing to say about your own output. Third instance of `schema-field-nothing-read`. Generalises: A SETTING AND ITS SUBJECT MUST ARRIVE TOGETHER. Asking for a bound while leaving the thing bounded undeclared produces a config that looks configured, and the tell is a question whose answer no code path can consume. Fixed: `init` declares its own stubs in `[memory].orientation` and merges rather than replaces whatever the operator confirmed about theirs; a new `orientation_shape` gate reports an orientation file with three or more DATED HEADINGS as a log becoming a 196 KB file, naming the companion to move it to (threshold three and headings only, because a gate firing on any date in an orientation file would be muted in a week); and the install skill gained a step that offers to restructure the project's own docs into that shape -- asking first, moving rather than rewriting their words, and declining to invent empty memory files.
2026-08-03 | rule-tested-against-its-author | derived-not-verified | gate | yes-now | `orientation_shape` shipped matching an ISO date ANYWHERE in a heading, on the reasoning that three dated headings in an always-loaded file means it has become a session log. It was tested against three synthetic documents I wrote myself, which agreed with the rule because the same hand wrote both. Its first contact with a real repo, minutes later, flagged the origin project's `design.md` -- a 40 KB DECISION REGISTER whose headings are subsystems with the decision date attached (`## Traps (decided 2026-07-17)`, `## Skills System (decided 2026-07-18, IMPLEMENTED WP1-WP8)`). That is precisely the shape an orientation document SHOULD have, and the gate's own docstring claimed to allow it -- in prose only, having never considered that the same construction appears in headings. Counted afterwards over all 30 memory documents: the shipped rule rejected 4 (two registers, wrongly; two history companions, out of scope), the corrected rule -- date must be at the START of the heading, which is what distinguishes 'the session is the subject' from 'the subsystem is the subject' -- rejects 1, a companion the gate never reads. Why it was believed: the rule was correct about the failure it was built from (`state.md` reaching 196 KB one dated section at a time), and a rule that explains its originating case feels verified by it. `.ai/recipes/verify-before-shipping-a-rule.md` exists for exactly this and I did not open it -- in the same hour I filed a card arguing that recipe should ship to consuming projects. Generalises: A RULE TESTED ONLY AGAINST EXAMPLES ITS AUTHOR WROTE HAS BEEN TESTED AGAINST ITS AUTHOR'S UNDERSTANDING, NOT AGAINST THE WORLD. The corpus is the test; step 4 of that recipe (report the count, zeros included -- how many pass, how many the rule REJECTS) would have caught this in one command, because 4-of-30 with two of them obviously fine is visible the moment it is printed. Fixed: the pattern anchors the date at the start of the heading text (leading `**`, `[`, `_` decoration allowed, since styling is not subject, and `(?!\d)` rather than `` to close it because `_2026-08-03_` has no word boundary); the reported companion is one the project already has rather than an invented `_history` name; the counted corpus is recorded in the gate's docstring; and the tests now include the register pattern verbatim from the real file.
2026-08-03 | gate-threshold-hid-the-backlog | derived-not-verified | maintainer | no-gap | The shipped gates are tuned to stop NEW mess, not to report the accumulated kind, and nothing in the install ever showed a repo what it already had. `dead_code` runs vulture at `min_confidence 80`, where vulture reports unused imports and variables but NOT unused functions, classes or methods -- those it rates at 60. Measured 2026-08-03 on a synthetic repo carrying a deliberately unused function: vulture at 60 reported it, the gate at 80 reported nothing, and the install ended green. The 80 default is right for a gate (it was chosen on the origin project, where 60 gives 74 findings and 80 gives 8) -- a gate at 60 would be muted in a week. The error was treating the day-to-day threshold as the only time anybody looks. Why it was believed: the install had grown an analysis step and a fix step, and both are about the state the checks describe; nobody asked what the checks are deliberately quiet about. Maintainer, 2026-08-03: 'New install should create the cards, so owner can check what is happening.' Generalises: A THRESHOLD TUNED FOR A RECURRING CHECK IS THE WRONG THRESHOLD FOR THE ONE-TIME SURVEY. The same tool answers two different questions -- 'has anything got worse since yesterday' and 'what is in here' -- and the second is asked exactly once, at install, when a noisy answer is affordable because a human is reading it. Fixed: the install skill gained a sweep step running vulture at 60 alongside the entry point (leaving it out reports everything only it uses as dead) and excluding tests/ (pytest injects fixtures by decorator, ~25 false findings), with the output becoming CARDS rather than a cleanup diff -- one card per cluster, each needing a real `## Approach` naming which symbols and whether anything reaches them dynamically. Unused imports are the single exception fixable in place, because a static tool cannot see a registry but it can see an import.
2026-08-04 | guard-denied-its-own-commit | derived-not-verified | claude | yes-now | `preflight_guard` was rewritten to resolve the pushed repo from the command instead of from the session, and shipped with a fail-closed path: anything it cannot attribute is denied. Correct policy. It was verified against 18 hand-piped payloads and 24 tests, every one of them a COMMAND -- `git push`, `git -C X push`, `cd X && git push`, `xargs git push`, an unterminated quote. None of them was a command whose PROSE mentions pushing. The first thing it met in real use was the commit of its own rewrite, whose message described the incident it was built for and contained the words `git push` and the apostrophe in `the session's repo`: the heredoc body made the command look guarded, the apostrophe made it unlexable, and it denied a `git commit` that pushed nothing. Then it denied the script written to investigate that, for the same reason. A here-document body is data the shell hands to a program's stdin and is never parsed as command text. Fixing that surfaced the mirror defect: `<<<` lexes as an operator the splitter does not know, so `cat <<<word` followed by a real push on the next line was ALLOWED -- caution pointed the wrong way in both directions at once. Why it was believed: the test corpus was drawn from the threat model (ways a push could hide) rather than from the traffic (what actually goes through a Bash hook all day), and every case in it passed. Second instance of `derived-not-verified` today, after `rule-tested-against-its-author`, and the same shape one layer out: there the examples were written by the rule's author, here they were drawn from the rule's own subject matter. Generalises: A GUARD IS TESTED AGAINST WHAT IT MUST CATCH AND LIVES AGAINST WHAT IT MUST LET THROUGH. The corpus that matters for a deny-by-default rule is the ordinary traffic, and for a hook on Bash that means commits, greps and echoes whose text discusses the very thing being guarded -- documentation of a rule is the input most likely to trip it. Fixed: heredoc bodies and here-strings are stripped before the guarded-op search and the lexer, three tests cover both directions including a push after a stripped body, and this commit message is itself the case that failed.
2026-08-04 | pre-existing-against-the-wrong-baseline | derived-not-verified | claude | no-judgment | A subagent finished the i18n-gate move and reported two failing Dungeoneer tests as 'pre-existing... I reproduced them with the framework stashed to HEAD, and they are about preflight_guard's receipt state, nothing I touched.' Both claims were wrong in the same way: HEAD of the framework ALREADY CONTAINED the guard rewrite committed an hour earlier, so stashing to HEAD reproduced the new behaviour, not the old. `test_guard_allows_when_it_cannot_parse` asserts the fail-open contract that rewrite deliberately reversed, and `test_guard_targets_the_merge_source_not_head` calls `_target_sha`, a function the rewrite renamed to `_sha` with a different signature -- an AttributeError, which is not a receipt-state failure by any reading. Caught because the test NAME contradicted a change I had made myself and remembered; had the rewrite been someone else's work a week earlier, the 'pre-existing' label would have stood and two tests would have sat red. Why it was believed: the agent's evidence procedure was sound in form -- stash, re-run, compare -- and its baseline was wrong, which no amount of care inside the procedure can detect. An editable install makes this specific trap easy: the framework's HEAD is live in the consuming project the instant it is committed, so 'before my change' and 'before any change' are different commits and only one of them is what `git stash` restores. Generalises: A CLEAN BISECT AGAINST THE WRONG BASELINE IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM A CLEAN BILL OF HEALTH. When a delegated result says 'pre-existing', the claim to check is which commit it called 'before', not whether it re-ran the test. Fixed: both tests updated to the current contract with the reversal explained in the docstring -- the fail-open one now states why blocking on confusion became correct once the guard resolves the target from the command rather than the session.
2026-08-04 | doctrine-from-recent-memory | derived-not-verified | claude | yes-now | The card said to ship the practice this project's tests follow: 'the docstring says what failure earned the test'. The agent counted before writing and the premise described a minority -- 345 of 533 framework test functions carry a docstring (64%) and 621 of 2027 in the origin project (30%). What IS universal is the MODULE docstring: 138 of 138 files in both suites. And in the sharpest case the bareness is the better practice -- one corpus-cache test file has 13 of 16 tests bare under a module docstring that says outright which are load-bearing and why. So the shipped rule became 'put the reasoning where the reasoning belongs' (the file carries it when one bug shaped the file, the test carries it when the test defends one incident) rather than the flat version the card asked for. Two more of the card's claims shrank under counting: 'the test that restates the implementation' turned up three instances across 2027 tests, all three defensible, so it was demoted from a numbered failure mode to a one-line Don't; and 'one behaviour per test' had to be worded as one behaviour rather than one assert, because the best test in the install suite makes five assertions that are all one behaviour. Why it was believed: the practice is real and visible in every test written in the last week -- which is the sample a person recalls, and recall is ordered by recency, not by frequency. The measurement the card was built from was 'the tests I have been reading', a corpus of maybe thirty. Generalises: A PRACTICE YOU CAN NAME FROM MEMORY IS A PRACTICE YOU HAVE SEEN RECENTLY, NOT ONE THE CORPUS FOLLOWS. Before shipping a convention as doctrine, count what fraction of the tree actually obeys it -- a rule that a third of the corpus violates is either a rule the corpus rejects or a backlog nobody has agreed to, and both are worth knowing before the rule is written down for other people. Same procedure as `verify-before-shipping-a-rule` step 2-4, applied to a practice rather than a schema. Fixed: the spine states the counted version, the two shrunken claims are demoted, and the summary-line failure mode -- which has three independent scars in production code -- was promoted into the slot the restated-implementation one vacated.
2026-08-04 | origin-identity-in-shipped-prompts | origin-identity-baked-in | maintainer | yes-now | `runner.py` sends five prompts to a model and FOUR carried the origin project. `_PROMPT` opened 'Execute one card from the Dungeoneer board' -- every worker, every card, every consuming project. `_REVIEW_PROMPT` routed on a named person who is not the maintainer, and told the reviewer not to re-check 'parity, imports, help-catalog overflow, asset hygiene': a game's gate list handed to a reviewer that will never see one of them. All four cited `00_architecture.md` SS13/SS16 as the authority for the tier they were running at -- a design note that deliberately does not ship, so the citation named a file the agent could not open. Four more runtime strings named the project or its maintainer, including a `commit_pathspec` denial that explained the operator's own repo to them in terms of somebody else's Obsidian habits. Counted by AST before touching anything, because most mentions are correct: 187 across 39 files, of which 83 docstrings and 58 comments are PROVENANCE -- a rule naming the incident that earned it -- and 6 were runtime strings. `templates/` was already clean at 0 because it renders a maintainer token, so the discipline existed in the codebase and stopped at the package boundary. Why it was believed: every one of those strings is TRUE in this repo. The framework checkout is the one place where naming the origin project reads as correct, so no gate, test or reviewer running here could see it -- the same blindness as `origin-repo-had-it-by-hand` but inverted, and one layer past `nightshift-reviewer-charter-is-game-shaped`, which fixed the same defect in a shipped .md a reader could at least notice. Generalises: A STRING THAT IS TRUE IN THE REPO THAT WROTE IT CANNOT BE AUDITED FROM THAT REPO. The test has to be 'would this sentence be false in a repo that is not ours', and the only mechanical proxy is the origin project's own name. Fixed: the four prompts state their rules in full instead of citing a doc that does not ship, the six runtime strings name roles rather than people, `doc_reference_liveness`/`doc_scan` prose that stated CURRENT BEHAVIOUR in origin terms was corrected while the 141 provenance mentions were deliberately left alone, and `tests/test_prompts_are_project_agnostic.py` fails on any runtime string naming the origin project or citing an `NN_*.md`. Kept as a test rather than a gate on purpose: a gate would ship into consuming projects where the rule is meaningless, which is `gate-scope-outlived-its-directory` written deliberately.
2026-08-04 | attributor-literal-was-a-silent-noop | origin-identity-baked-in | test | yes-now | Found by the test written for the entry above, not by reading. `digest._KAREL_ANSWER` matched the literal token `karel` after the `·` in a `## Thread` heading, to flag a card that was answered but never moved out of `needs-decision/`. In every repo but the origin's that advisory ran, matched nothing, and reported a clean board -- and a nudge that never fires is indistinguishable from a board with nothing to nudge about. Nothing in the suite touched the advisory at all, which is how a literal first name survived the extraction. The interesting part is what the fix could NOT be: counted over the origin project's 62 `### <date> · <token>` headings, the bare single-word attributors are `karel` (24), `triage` (10), `code-thread` and `claude` -- so a shape rule ('a single bare word means a human decided') reads three agents' own notes as a recorded decision. The name is doing the work, so the project has to declare it. Why it was believed: the regex's comment argued carefully about matching NARROWLY -- why `interactive session (Karel + Claude)` must not trip it -- and reasoning that hard about the shape of the match makes the identity of the token feel like part of the shape. Generalises: A CHECK KEYED ON A PROPER NOUN IS OFF EVERYWHERE THAT NOUN IS NOT, AND OFF LOOKS EXACTLY LIKE CLEAN. When a rule matches a name rather than a form, ask which repos it is a no-op in and whether anything would say so. Fixed: `[board].decision_attributor`, CONFIRM-tier in discovery (guessed from git user.name's first word, never HIGH, empty disables the advisory rather than falling back to a guess), the digest compiles the pattern from it, and the shipped `manage-board` skill renders the same token so the WRITE side and the READ side of the convention cannot drift. 11 tests, including every attributor shape from the real corpus. The templates-carry-no-origin-identity test was widened from `ai/recipes/` to the whole template tree, which is what caught the skill telling every project to sign `· karel`.
2026-08-04 | kanban-needed-a-plugin-nobody-named | origin-repo-had-it-by-hand | maintainer | yes-now | The shipped `Board/README.md` said the board view 'needs the **Bases** core plugin, which ships with Obsidian 1.9 and later'. True, and not sufficient: core Bases provides table/cards/list, while `type: kanban` -- the view the same paragraph is selling, with a row in its own table reading 'one column per lane, cards draggable between them' -- comes from the **Base Board** COMMUNITY plugin. So the documented path lands on `Unknown view type: kanban`, and the operator has followed the instructions exactly. Reported by the maintainer on their second install, 2026-08-04: *"I opened the Obsidian, installed base board ... but it tells me 'Unknown view type: kanban' when I open the Board"*. Confirmed by reading `.obsidian/community-plugins.json` in the origin checkout: `base-board` v2.4.1 has been enabled there the whole time. Why it was believed: the doc was written while fixing `board-had-no-view`, by an author whose vault already rendered the kanban -- so the plugin question that got thought about was the one that was still open (does Bases ship with Obsidian, and from which version), and the dependency that was already satisfied was never a question at all. FOURTH instance of `origin-repo-had-it-by-hand` and the SECOND about this one view, which is the tell: the first fix shipped the file and the second had to ship the thing that renders it. Generalises: A DEPENDENCY THAT WAS ALREADY INSTALLED WHEN YOU WROTE THE DOC IS NOT A DEPENDENCY YOU WILL REMEMBER TO NAME. The check is not 'did I list the requirements' but 'what is enabled on this machine that the reader's machine will not have' -- for anything with a GUI, read the app's own plugin/extension config rather than recalling it. Corollary applied here: name the ERROR STRING, not just the fix, because the person with the symptom searches for the symptom. Fixed: the board README splits the two plugins and names `Unknown view type: kanban`; the `.base` header says the same at the point `type: kanban` is declared; `init`'s closing screen carries it as the one step neither of its two commands can do, since it is a click in another application; the install skill's handover step must state it; the framework README gained the split. One test asserts all three shipped docs name both `Base Board` and the error string.
2026-08-04 | shipped-the-view-not-the-switch | work-printed-instead-of-done | maintainer | no-gap | The fix for `kanban-needed-a-plugin-nobody-named` was four documents telling the operator which two plugins to enable. Correct, and still the framework declining to do its own job: BOTH toggles live in `.obsidian/`, a directory `init` can read and write, so the instruction was work printed instead of done. The maintainer, 2026-08-04: *"As plugins are declared in .obsidian, can you add this plugin to it's settings so Obsidian loads it? Also a user can already have Obsidian over the folder, so we can't just overwrite, we need to append during install."* Both halves matter and the second is the harder one: `.obsidian/` predates us in any vault opened once and holds themes, hotkeys, window layout and every other plugin they run. Why it was believed: the previous entry's finding was framed as a DOCUMENTATION defect -- the README named one plugin and needed to name two -- so the fix inherited that frame and never asked whether the thing being documented was actionable. Generalises: WHEN A DOC FIX TELLS SOMEBODY TO CHANGE A FILE YOU CAN ALREADY WRITE, THE DOC WAS THE WRONG ARTEFACT. Second instance of `work-printed-instead-of-done` after `install-ended-with-homework`, and the same tell -- an imperative addressed to the reader about state the tool owns. Fixed: `_plan_obsidian` merges `base-board` into `community-plugins.json` and `bases: true` into `core-plugins.json`, both read-modify-write with every other key preserved. The two files are treated ASYMMETRICALLY on purpose: an absent community list means 'none enabled' so creating it adds nothing and is safe, while `core-plugins.json` maps EVERY core plugin to a boolean, so writing a partial one is not an append but a claim about plugins nobody asked about -- merged if present, never created. Unreadable either file: refuse and say so, never rewrite. No vault at all: do nothing and note it, because manufacturing `.obsidian/` decides something that is the operator's. Enabling is not installing, so the info line says NOT installed and names Browse when the plugin directory is absent. `.obsidian/` is excluded from the receipt: by uninstall time 'did nightshift enable Bases or did they' is unanswerable, and switching off a plugin somebody has used for months to tidy up after ourselves is the worse error. 10 tests plus a run against a vault carrying dataview, templater, a theme and four core plugins -- all preserved.
2026-08-04 | explanation-shipped-into-a-file-a-plugin-rewrites | derived-not-verified | claude | yes-now | The same fix put the plugin explanation into `templates/board.base`'s comment header, and asserted it there with a test. Obsidian's Bases plugin normalises that file every time the vault opens and its normalisation DROPS YAML COMMENTS, so the header reaches nobody: measured 2026-08-04 in the origin project, whose live `Board.base` carries 0 comment lines against the template's 12. The test was green throughout, because it read the TEMPLATE -- the artefact nobody's Obsidian touches -- while the delivered file lost the text on first launch. A test verifying the wrong artefact is worse than no test: it reports coverage of a property that does not hold in production. Reported by an agent in another project, which noticed the rewrite as an unexplained diff and left it uncommitted. Why it was believed: `.base` is a file this tool writes, in this tool's format, from this tool's template -- three true things that together imply ownership, and the owner is whichever program last wrote it. Generalises: A FILE ANOTHER PROGRAM NORMALISES IS NOT A PLACE TO PUT ANYTHING THAT MUST SURVIVE, and the test for durable content has to read the artefact AS DELIVERED, not the template it came from. The tell is any file a GUI opens and saves. Fixed: the header stays but now says it will vanish and names the durable copy; the docs test drops `board.base` from the list it checks and a new test pins only that self-describing claim, with the 0-vs-12 count in its docstring. Also the maintainer's standing rule, which the reporting agent got backwards: an Obsidian rewrite is expected, not damage -- *"obsidian changes should not cause any problem and should be commited with every commit"* -- so both copies of `manage-board` now forbid treating one as a finding, leaving it uncommitted, or restoring the comments.
2026-08-04 | looking-at-it-broke-it | origin-repo-had-it-by-hand | maintainer | yes-now | A consuming project could not dispatch a card because `.obsidian/workspace.json` was dirty -- reported THREE TIMES in one day, each time after the previous fix. The chain is three links and every one is ours: `templates/gitignore` said nothing about `.obsidian/`; `init`'s closing screen says `git add -A && git commit`; and the same morning `init` started WRITING into `.obsidian/`, which makes the directory read as something you are meant to commit. So the vault config gets swept in, including `workspace.json` -- which Obsidian rewrites when you open a pane, switch tabs or scroll -- and `dirty_outside_board` then refuses every dispatch. OPENING THE BOARD TO READ IT IS WHAT STOPS THE BOARD BEING WORKED, and each look re-dirties it, so the state is not recoverable by committing. Why it was believed: the origin project has had `.obsidian/` in its own `.gitignore` since long before the extraction, so no run here could ever produce the failure -- and today's three Obsidian fixes all went to the same blind spot without any of them asking the one question that repo answers by hand. Fifth instance of `origin-repo-had-it-by-hand` and the third about this one directory: shipping the view, then the plugins that render it, then the rule that keeps looking at it from being destructive. Generalises: WHEN A FIX MAKES A TOOL WRITE SOMEWHERE NEW, THE QUESTION IS NOT ONLY 'IS THE WRITE CORRECT' BUT 'WHAT ELSE LIVES THERE, AND WHAT HAPPENS WHEN IT IS COMMITTED'. Adding a write to a directory adopts the whole directory's churn. Fixed in two places on purpose, because they cover different repos: `templates/gitignore` names `workspace.json` and `workspace-mobile.json` -- deliberately NOT `.obsidian/` wholesale, since community-plugins.json and core-plugins.json are real shared config that `init` writes precisely so a second machine need not redo the setup -- and `dirty_outside_board` now skips `.obsidian/` entirely, which is what rescues a repo that tracked one before the ignore rule shipped. The skip is safe because the reason for refusing does not apply: no worktree, gate, test or card reads a byte of vault state, and it is the `Board/` exemption's own rationale one directory over -- somebody looking at the board while a session runs. `init` also reports a tracked workspace file with the two-command untrack, since no `.gitignore` rule can fix what git already has. 9 tests, including that a vault rewrite and a source edit in the same tree still refuses and still names the source edit.
2026-08-06 | reuse-cache-merge-base-self-diffed | derived-what-only-the-act-knew | claude | no-gap | Card `empty-diff-preflight-runs-everything` left an adjacent observation deliberately unexplained: two consecutive preflights at `6bd5665` and `948a1ac` in the Dungeoneer repo, differing only in a Board-only commit (a `kanban_order` renumber), both re-ran the full 693-test system slice with no part reused, even though a board-only path folds out of the system fingerprint by design. `preflight._run_pytest`'s reuse cache built its env tag from a SECOND, independent `git merge-base HEAD <base>` call -- separate from the one `_changed_paths`/`_diff_base` already resolves for the diff itself. `_diff_base` redirects to `origin/<base>` when `HEAD` IS `base`, which is routine when board/plan commits land straight on the integration branch (the exact shape of the two commits above); the reuse cache's own call used the literal branch name instead, so on that path `git merge-base HEAD base` resolved to HEAD's own SHA -- a value that changes on every commit -- and that went straight into `_env_tag`, hashed into every part's fingerprint. CONFIRMED, not derived from reading: I built a synthetic repo to the same shape (two commits landed directly on an integration branch, a fixed `origin/<base>` behind both, the second commit touching only a `Board/`-classified path) and measured the two merge-base values differ across commits under the old code and agree under the fix, with the system part's fingerprint then matching byte-for-byte between runs and the cache actually hitting on the second one. Why it was believed correct: `git merge-base HEAD <base>` reads as the obviously right call at the point it was written, and it IS right for every branch that is not the integration branch itself -- the one case it silently breaks is exactly the case `_diff_base` exists to redirect, three functions away in the same file. Generalises: A FACT ALREADY RESOLVED CORRECTLY ONCE, ELSEWHERE IN THE SAME MODULE, AND THEN RECOMPUTED NAIVELY BY A SECOND CALL SITE IS INDISTINGUISHABLE FROM THE ORIGINAL UNTIL THE INPUT HITS THE CASE THE FIRST CALL SITE WAS BUILT TO HANDLE -- the fix is not a smarter second computation, it is not having a second one. The card's own warning earned its keep here: the first pass toward this observation reached for a fingerprint-granularity theory before anything had been run, and only measuring (checkout a real commit, call the real functions, compare the real hashes) surfaced the actual cause. Fixed: `_changed_paths` now returns the merge-base SHA it already resolves, as a third tuple element; `_run_pytest` and `run_checks` thread that value through instead of recomputing it. `tests/test_preflight_empty_diff.py::test_the_reuse_cache_hits_across_two_board_only_commits_on_the_base_branch` pins the exact two-commit scenario measured. [[disposition: commit: nightshift preflight.py -- _changed_paths returns the resolved merge-base, _run_pytest and run_checks reuse it instead of recomputing]]
2026-08-07 | check-ignore-probed-as-a-file | derived-not-verified | test | no-gap | `source_reference_liveness._gitignored` probed each candidate exactly as the source wrote it, so `.ai/runs` never matched this repo's own `.gitignore` entry `.ai/runs/`: a trailing-slash pattern matches DIRECTORIES ONLY, and `git check-ignore` cannot infer directory-ness for a path that does not exist on disk. Ten violations across runner.py, digest.py and hooks/correction_prompt.py -- on the exact path the module docstring names as the worked example of its own structural pass, so the doc and the code disagreed and the doc was believed. Believed because `git check-ignore <path>` reads as a total predicate, when its answer actually depends on a trailing slash AND on disk state; the pass was reasoned out against a path that exists, while the entire population reaching this code is paths that do NOT exist yet, which is why they reach it. Generalises: when shelling out to a tool for a yes/no, reproduce the condition the real population has rather than the convenient one -- and when a docstring names a specific worked example, that example is a testable claim, not prose. Failure direction is the bad one: the gate looked STRICTER than it was and errored nowhere, so ten false positives read as genuine debt. Caught by test_self_gating, a test not written for this. [[disposition: commit: a0b783c]]
2026-08-10 | perf-number-taken-from-one-cold-run | derived-not-verified | maintainer | no-gap | The Czech rewrite of docs/index.html asserted the gate suite runs "~18 s, after every file edit", replacing the old deck's "~7 s" as a stale figure corrected. Both timings were real: I measured 18.9 s and 17.5 s and treated two agreeing numbers as a measurement. Karel disbelieved it -- "that grew again after we reduced it" -- and re-measuring showed the suite runs 6.5-6.8 s warm and spikes to ~18-21 s sporadically, with the spread spread evenly across all 39 gates (deletion_sweep alone swings 0.26 s to 5.61 s), i.e. machine variance, not the suite. In-process the checks total 6.6 s against the 4.14 s recorded when corpus.py landed at 31 gates -- eight more gates for +1.5 s, no regression, which is the answer his question actually deserved. Why it was believed: repeating a measurement feels like verifying it, and two cold runs back to back are ONE observation of a bimodal distribution, not two. The number was also the kind nobody challenges in a slide, and it was going in front of colleagues as evidence the tool is honest about itself. Generalises: A PERFORMANCE NUMBER IS A DISTRIBUTION, NOT A VALUE -- repeating a measurement back to back samples the same machine state, so a figure that will be published needs runs separated by other work, and the range reported rather than the mode. Corollary found while checking: the PostToolUse hook budget is 30 s against a worst measured 21.5 s (72 %), and the hook ends `; exit 0`, so crossing it makes the gates vanish silently -- the same shape as the 33.8 s-against-15 s incident already in this log, still unguarded. Deck now says ~7 s; the headroom finding is reported to Karel, not fixed here. [[disposition: doc: docs/index.html gate timing corrected to ~7 s]]
2026-08-10 | probe-read-the-wrong-true-number | derived-not-verified | maintainer | no-judgment | `suite.available_memory_gb` returned `ullAvailPhys` -- free PHYSICAL memory -- to decide how many xdist workers a box can afford. The number is real, the field is real, the reading is correct, and it is the wrong quantity: what `-n auto` exhausts on Windows is the system-wide COMMIT CHARGE, and commit is charged when a page is reserved rather than when it is first touched, so free-RAM and commit-headroom diverge without bound. Reported by Karel as the Dungeoneer suite crashing VS Code and other unrelated apps while "RAM was only at 52%". Measured on that box: 18.9 GB free RAM, 13.4 GB of commit headroom against a 127.9 GB limit (89.5% committed, cross-checked against `\Memory\% Committed Bytes In Use` and Win32_PerfFormattedData_PerfOS_Memory). `worker_count(12, 18.9)` found 12 workers affordable and therefore DECLINED TO CAP; 12 workers wanted ~16.8 GB of commit. The 2026-07-31 cap, built for exactly this failure, was present, correct-looking, fully unit-tested and inert. Two properties kept it invisible and both generalise past this function. (1) The failure is not attributable to the thing that caused it: commit exhaustion is system-wide, so the allocation that fails belongs to whichever process asks next -- the suite ran green to completion and the DESKTOP died, which is not a shape anyone debugging a test runner goes looking for, and the meter a human checks (Task Manager's memory percentage) is physical and reads normal throughout. (2) Every test of the cap injected `memory_gb` as a literal to keep `worker_count` pure. That purity is right and it moved the only untested statement in the feature -- the measurement -- to the one place nothing was looking: 100% coverage of the arithmetic, zero coverage of which field was read. THE TEST THAT MATTERS FOR A FUNCTION THAT READS THE PLATFORM IS THE ONE ASSERTING IT READ THE RIGHT NUMBER, NOT THE ONES EXERCISING WHAT IT DOES WITH IT. Fixed: probe returns min(physical, commit headroom) on Windows (POSIX unchanged -- default overcommit has no comparable hard limit); new `SYSTEM_RESERVE_GB = 4.0` subtracted before dividing, because the corrected probe ALONE still allowed 9 workers = 12.6 of 13.4 GB and left ~0.8 GB for the editor the tests were launched from; new test calls `GlobalMemoryStatusEx` directly and asserts the probe tracks the scarcer meter. Not gated: no gate can see a machine's commit headroom, and which platform field is semantically correct is judgment, not pattern-matching -- the durable guard here is the test, not a gate. Consuming-project half (root `conftest.py` routing a hand-typed `pytest -n auto` through this probe at all, which nothing did) is logged in Dungeoneer as `guard-watched-the-wrong-meter`. [[disposition: commit: 889ec19 -- probe reads the scarcer meter, SYSTEM_RESERVE_GB added, probe-field test added]]
